window.__BLOG&&window.__BLOG('g2',"<h1 class=\"btitle\">Helping Without Being Told: Embodied Helpers, Partner Pools and the Benchmarks Around Them</h1><p class=\"bby\">Qihang Li · October 2026 · written from the reading-map notes of this tree</p><nav class=\"btoc\" aria-label=\"Sections\"><button class=\"lnk\" data-go=\"0\">Setting: a helper that has to guess what you want</button><button class=\"lnk\" data-go=\"1\">Reading the partner: from demos to goals to capabilities</button><button class=\"lnk\" data-go=\"2\">From partner pools to platforms</button><button class=\"lnk\" data-go=\"3\">Language, constraints and new data</button><button class=\"lnk\" data-go=\"4\">How the line is measured, and why the numbers do not line up</button><button class=\"lnk\" data-go=\"5\">Open problems, and a reading order</button></nav><div class=\"bbody\"><h2 id=\"b-setting-a-helper-that-has-to-guess-what-you-want\">Setting: a helper that has to guess what you want</h2>\n<p>Picture a kitchen. You pull the bread out of the cupboard and walk toward the fridge. A household robot is watching, and it has about a second to decide: fetch the butter, clear the counter, or stay out of the way. Nobody told it that you are making a sandwich. Add a twist: the jam is on a shelf you cannot reach. A good helper brings the jam and leaves the plates alone, because doing what you could do yourself is clutter, not help.</p>\n<p>This post is about the line of work that tries to build and test that robot. I read thirteen papers for it. They do not form one clean lineage; what they share is a <em>setting</em>: an embodied agent (a robot, or an avatar in a simulated home) must help a partner whose goal, and sometimes whose abilities, it cannot see.</p>\n<p>Let's define the pieces. The <strong>main agent</strong> is the partner being helped: a person, or a scripted or learned stand-in for one. The <strong>helper</strong> is the agent we are building. The main agent has a hidden <strong>type</strong> <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta = (g, \\eta)</annotation></semantics></math></span>: a goal <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math></span> (which objects should end up where) and, in the later papers, capabilities <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta</annotation></semantics></math></span> (how high it can reach, what it can lift). Here is a simplified version of the problem that the papers below instantiate in different ways:</p>\n<div class=\"mdisp\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></munder><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mo>∣</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">[</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>t</mi></munder><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\max_{\\pi_H}\\ \\mathbb{E}_{\\theta \\sim p(\\theta)}\\ \\mathbb{E}_{\\tau \\sim (\\pi_M(\\cdot \\mid \\theta),\\, \\pi_H)}\\Big[\\sum_t r_t\\Big]</annotation></semantics></math></span></div>\n<p>The helper policy <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_H</annotation></semantics></math></span> sees only what its sensors give it, and the main agent's policy <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_M</annotation></semantics></math></span> depends on the hidden <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The trajectory <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> is generated jointly, and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_t</annotation></semantics></math></span> is a reward that the papers define differently: a shared team reward (<button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"106\" title=\"Social rearrange · Adaptive Coordination in Social Embodied Rearrangement\">Social rearrange</button>, <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"108\" title=\"Habitat 3.0 · Habitat 3.0: A Co-Habitat for Humans, Avatars and Robots\">Habitat 3.0</button>, <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"90\" title=\"CoELA · Building Cooperative Embodied Agents Modularly with Large Language Models\">CoELA</button>), or a score for being useful to the main agent (<button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"102\" title=\"Watch-And-Help · Watch-And-Help: A Challenge for Social Perception and Human-AI Collaboration\">Watch-And-Help</button>, <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"85\" title=\"Smart Help · Smart Help: Strategic Opponent Modeling for Proactive and Adaptive Robot Assistance in Households\">Smart Help</button>). This is my own simplification, not any one paper's formulation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-why-it-is-hard\">Why it is hard</h3>\n<p>Four things make this setting hard.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The goal is hidden.</strong> The helper reads it only through the partner's actions, and many actions fit many goals.</li>\n<li><strong>Partners differ in ability.</strong> A helper that assumes an equally capable partner will help in the wrong places.</li>\n<li><strong>The world is a 3D home.</strong> Perception, navigation and manipulation must work before social reasoning matters, so many papers give the helper perfect low-level skills.</li>\n<li><strong>People are expensive to test with.</strong> Most partners are scripts, planners, trained pools or language models, and human studies are small.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"b-what-existing-ideas-leave-open\">What existing ideas leave open</h3>\n<p>The papers cite four families of earlier work, and each leaves a gap. Imitation methods in the style of MA-GAIL and InfoGAIL learn from demonstrations, while multi-agent RL needs hand-made rewards (cited by <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"58\" title=\"Co-GAIL · Co-GAIL: Learning Diverse Strategies for Human-Robot Collaboration\">Co-GAIL</button>). Simulators such as VirtualHome, Habitat 2.0 and ALFRED gave homes and tasks, but for one agent only. Theory-of-Mind and inverse-planning work guessed goals, but on toy grids (Baker et al., cited by <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"102\" title=\"Watch-And-Help · Watch-And-Help: A Challenge for Social Perception and Human-AI Collaboration\">Watch-And-Help</button>). And population-based training (PBT) and <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"59\" title=\"FCP · Collaborating with Humans without Human Data\">FCP</button> train a pool of partners, but on 2D Overcooked, a small kitchen game. The papers below fill these gaps in turn.</p>\n<p>Two more terms. <strong>Zero-shot coordination (ZSC)</strong> means teaming up with partners never seen in training, with no fine-tuning; it is the test used by the pool-based papers, and the population-training line it comes from is covered in its own post (the sibling tree on <em>Population training for humans</em>). <strong>HITL</strong>, human-in-the-loop, means a real person drives a simulated avatar during evaluation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-the-thirteen-papers-in-one-picture\">The thirteen papers in one picture</h3>\n<p>The tree's own notes describe 25 citation edges, of which 6 are inheritance and 19 are comparisons (related-work citations or benchmark-table entries). Most of the thirteen papers are therefore connected by <em>topic</em>, not by reuse. The stage grouping and the two-thread framing below are my own synthesis. In arrow form, the two chains with real inheritance are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Watch-And-Help → <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"80\" title=\"NOPA · NOPA: Neurally-guided Online Probabilistic Assistance for Building Socially Intelligent Home Assistants\">NOPA</button> (drop the demonstration, infer the goal online)</li>\n<li>Watch-And-Help → CoELA → <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"110\" title=\"CHAIC · Constrained Human-AI Cooperation: An Inclusive Embodied Social Intelligence Challenge\">CHAIC</button> (reuse the environment, then the framework, for constrained partners)</li>\n<li>FCP → Social rearrange → Habitat 3.0 → <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"112\" title=\"PARTNR · PARTNR: A Benchmark for Planning and Reasoning in Embodied Multi-agent Tasks\">PARTNR</button> (partner pools, then a platform, then a benchmark)</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"bfig\"><img src=\"assets/img/g2_method.webp\" alt=\"Evolution of the tree: arrows run from a work to the works that build on it.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Evolution of the tree: arrows run from a work to the works that build on it.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The figure shows only these inheritance edges. Two chains emerge: a \"help and LLM agents\" chain rooted in Watch-And-Help, and a \"pools to platform\" chain rooted in FCP. The five papers that do not appear in it, Co-GAIL, <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"104\" title=\"HandMeThat · HandMeThat: Human-Robot Communication in Physical and Social Environments\">HandMeThat</button>, <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"107\" title=\"HoloAssist · HoloAssist: an Egocentric Human Interaction Dataset for Interactive AI Assistants in the Real World\">HoloAssist</button>, Smart Help and <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"99\" title=\"RoboFactory · RoboFactory: Exploring Embodied Agent Collaboration with Compositional Constraints\">RoboFactory</button>, are linked only by comparison or topic.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Paper</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>What it contributes</th>\n<th>How it joins this tree</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Co-GAIL</td>\n<td>2021</td>\n<td>Robot reads a human's latent strategy, learned from demos</td>\n<td>Topic only (cited by two members)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FCP (guest)</td>\n<td>2021</td>\n<td>Pool of past selves, then best response</td>\n<td>Parent of Social rearrange</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Watch-And-Help</td>\n<td>2021</td>\n<td>Challenge, platform, goal inference</td>\n<td>Parent of NOPA and CoELA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HandMeThat</td>\n<td>2022</td>\n<td>Benchmark for vague requests</td>\n<td>Compared to Watch-And-Help</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NOPA</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>Online goal inference plus inverse planning</td>\n<td>Child of Watch-And-Help</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Social rearrange</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>Diverse partner pool in 3D homes</td>\n<td>Child of FCP, parent of Habitat 3.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HoloAssist</td>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>166 hours of real guided tasks</td>\n<td>Topic only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Smart Help</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>Infers goal and capability</td>\n<td>Baseline links to Watch-And-Help, NOPA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CoELA (guest)</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>LLM modules that plan and talk</td>\n<td>Child of Watch-And-Help, parent of CHAIC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Habitat 3.0</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>Humanoid and robot simulator</td>\n<td>Child of Social rearrange</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CHAIC</td>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>Constrained partners benchmark</td>\n<td>Child of CoELA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RoboFactory</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>Checked multi-arm demonstrations</td>\n<td>Table-only links</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PARTNR</td>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td>100,000 LLM-made tasks</td>\n<td>Child of Habitat 3.0</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"b-reading-the-partner-from-demos-to-goals-to-capab\">Reading the partner: from demos to goals to capabilities</h2>\n<p>The first group of papers asks the question the opening picture raises: what does the partner want, and what can it do? The usual answer is to infer, then plan.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-co-gail-a-robot-learns-each-human-s-hidden-strat\">Co-GAIL: a robot learns each human's hidden strategy</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"58\" title=\"Co-GAIL · Co-GAIL: Learning Diverse Strategies for Human-Robot Collaboration\">Co-GAIL</button> (Wang et al., CoRL 2021) works from human-human demonstrations, with no hand-made reward. One co-policy outputs both the human's and the robot's actions, conditioned on a latent strategy code <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math></span>; the robot reads <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math></span> from the human's recent motion. An extra loss forces the policy to use <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and another reconstructs the demonstrated human actions so that rare strategies are not collapsed. The setting is narrow: fixed leader and follower roles, one human and one robot, simulation only.</p>\n<p>On replay success (Table 1, mean of 3 seeds, three tasks), Co-GAIL scores 53.3, 43.9 and 40.0% against 21.1, 13.3 and 15.0% for a single behavior-cloned policy. With 4 real users and 20 trials per method per task (Table 3), Co-GAIL is best in 11 of 12 user-task cases (my count; the paper says all but one). One caveat from the paper's own Table 2: interpolation success is lower for Co-GAIL on two of the three tasks (for example 81.2 vs 99.2 for MA-GAIL on handover). No member of the tree builds on it; <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"106\" title=\"Social rearrange · Adaptive Coordination in Social Embodied Rearrangement\">Social rearrange</button> and <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"108\" title=\"Habitat 3.0 · Habitat 3.0: A Co-Habitat for Humans, Avatars and Robots\">Habitat 3.0</button> mention it only in related work.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-watch-and-help-the-challenge-that-the-rest-of-th\">Watch-And-Help: the challenge that the rest of the chain grows from</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"102\" title=\"Watch-And-Help · Watch-And-Help: A Challenge for Social Perception and Human-AI Collaboration\">Watch-And-Help</button> (WAH; Puig et al., ICLR 2021) is the root of the helping chain. Its challenge has two stages. In <em>Watch</em>, the helper Bob sees one demonstration of Alice pursuing a hidden goal and must infer it. In <em>Help</em>, Bob is placed in a different environment and must help Alice reach the same goal faster. A goal is 2 to 8 predicates over 30 predicate types, across five household activities, and both agents have partial symbolic views. The paper contributes the challenge, a platform called VirtualHome-Social (with a built-in human-like Alice and a human interface), and baselines: a goal-inference network and helpers built from hierarchical MCTS or reinforcement learning (1,011 training tasks, two test sets of 100).</p>\n<p>The paper gives no single headline; my pick is the Watch stage. On test set 1, goal inference reaches precision 0.85 and recall 0.96, against 0.08 and 0.09 for chance. Using only the last observation falls to 0.79 and 0.75, and handing the model Alice's actions gives 0.99 and 0.99, so how much of Alice's behavior the model sees matters a great deal. On test set 2, which mixes two activities, precision and recall drop to 0.68 and 0.64. In the Help stage, planning-based helpers are best; a planner that acts on a <em>random</em> goal causes conflicts in 40% of episodes, and the reinforcement-learning helper is no better than a random one. One oddity I report as printed: on test 2, Alice alone succeeds 95.4% of the time and the helper-assisted team 88.6% (speedup 0.21).</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-nopa-guess-the-goal-online-then-check-it-by-inve\">NOPA: guess the goal online, then check it by inverse planning</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"80\" title=\"NOPA · NOPA: Neurally-guided Online Probabilistic Assistance for Building Socially Intelligent Home Assistants\">NOPA</button> (Puig et al., ICRA 2023) extends the same task and reuses WAH's hierarchical planner, but removes the demonstration: the helper must infer the goal while Alice acts. A neural network looks at the change in object counts between the first and current states and proposes <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K = 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> candidate goals. For each, MCTS simulates 15 steps of Alice, and any candidate whose simulated behavior contradicts what Alice actually did is rejected (this is the inverse-planning filter). The helper then picks subgoals by how likely they are to be needed times the time they save. Training uses 6,000 solo episodes (300 in a small-data variant); test uses 100 episodes in two unseen apartments.</p>\n<p>Let's define <strong>speedup</strong> as the main agent's solo episode length divided by the team's length, minus one (WAH's definition). Every NOPA speedup is read off a plot (about <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pm 0.02</annotation></semantics></math></span>): roughly 0.44 for NOPA against 0.34 for the best baseline, and roughly 0.34 without the inverse-planning filter. So the filter accounts for about 0.10 of speedup (my arithmetic, from approximate values). With 10 human participants the plotted values are about 0.42 against 0.20 for the strongest baseline. An oracle that knows the goal reaches 1.29, far above the plot.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-smart-help-also-infer-what-the-partner-cannot-do\">Smart Help: also infer what the partner cannot do</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"85\" title=\"Smart Help · Smart Help: Strategic Opponent Modeling for Proactive and Adaptive Robot Assistance in Households\">Smart Help</button> (Cao et al., arXiv 2024) adds the second hidden thing, capability. The user has a goal and a six-number capability vector (reach height, liftable weight, and the abilities to open, close and toggle objects); the user is a scripted expert with one reduced capability, in AI2-THOR kitchens. An opponent-modeling module infers goal and capability, and an RL helper (PPO, LSTM actor-critic) is rewarded for the user's goals and <em>penalized by 30</em> when it finishes a goal the user could have done alone. The paper links to WAH and NOPA only by baseline replication and related work; I do not claim method inheritance.</p>\n<p>On 14 test cases over 10 held-out kitchens (3 seeds), task success is 48.3% for the full model, against 34.5% for an LLM helper (gpt-3.5) and 27.4% for a heuristic MCTS helper; an oracle MCTS that knows the true goal reaches 59.3%. The penalty barely changes success (0.488 without, 0.483 with) but raises a \"helping necessity\" score from 0.455 to 0.498, which is the effect the design aims at. The full model is not best on every metric, and there is no human study.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Paper</th>\n<th>What is hidden</th>\n<th>Who plays the partner</th>\n<th>Evidence in its own paper</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Co-GAIL</td>\n<td>Strategy code</td>\n<td>Simulated human; 4 real users</td>\n<td>Replay success, 3 seeds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Watch-And-Help</td>\n<td>Goal (one demo given)</td>\n<td>Planner Alice, or a human via interface</td>\n<td>Precision / recall, speedup plots</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NOPA</td>\n<td>Goal, online</td>\n<td>Built-in planner; 10 humans</td>\n<td>Speedup (read off plots)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Smart Help</td>\n<td>Goal and capability</td>\n<td>Scripted expert</td>\n<td>Success rate, 14 test cases</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"b-from-partner-pools-to-platforms\">From partner pools to platforms</h2>\n<p>The second chain starts outside the home. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"59\" title=\"FCP · Collaborating with Humans without Human Data\">FCP</button> was built for Overcooked, a game of two cooks in a small 2D kitchen. I give it a short recap here because its home is the <em>Population training for humans</em> tree, which tells the story properly.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-fcp-the-recipe-that-the-chain-borrows\">FCP: the recipe that the chain borrows</h3>\n<p>FCP (Strouse et al., NeurIPS 2021, guest here) trains 32 self-play agents that differ only in random seed, saves three checkpoints of each (initialization, 50% of final reward, end), and trains a single agent as the best response to that frozen pool; 32 × 3 = 96 partners (my arithmetic). With a human proxy, FCP averages 10.6 deliveries over five layouts against 4.7 for the same method without checkpoints (Table 1, 5 seeds). A study with 114 online participants has FCP beating self-play, population play and a human-model baseline (p &lt; 0.001) and preferred (p &lt; 0.05). The paper admits that the reward is known and fixed, partners are chosen by hand, and the pool may be unrealistically large for harder games. It earns a place here because <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"106\" title=\"Social rearrange · Adaptive Coordination in Social Embodied Rearrangement\">Social rearrange</button> inherits its two-stage scheme.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-social-rearrange-diversity-from-a-discriminator-\">Social rearrange: diversity from a discriminator, in 3D homes</h3>\n<p>Social rearrange (Szot et al., ICML 2023) keeps FCP's skeleton (build a partner population, then train a coordinator against it) but argues that random-seed diversity is not enough, and moves the setting to Habitat homes with two Fetch robots, no communication, 256×256 depth views and perfect, fixed low-level skills. Its method, which the paper calls <strong>BDP</strong>, trains one latent-conditioned policy that plays 8 different partner behaviors. A trajectory discriminator, looking at the robot's position and its last 40 actions, rewards behavior that is easy to tell apart (weight 0.01). A coordinator is then trained for 100M steps against the frozen partners and tested on 3 scripted and 8 learned held-out partners. The discriminator matters: removing it halves success on Set Table (22.92 against 46.43).</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>ZSC success, 3 seeds (Table 1)</th>\n<th>Set Table</th>\n<th>Tidy House</th>\n<th>Prepare Groceries</th>\n<th>Average (my arithmetic)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>BDP</td>\n<td>46.43</td>\n<td>66.71</td>\n<td>75.85</td>\n<td>63.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PBT</td>\n<td>30.34</td>\n<td>56.88</td>\n<td>56.08</td>\n<td>47.8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FCP</td>\n<td>37.50</td>\n<td>34.07</td>\n<td>30.00</td>\n<td>33.9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Oracle with privileged state</td>\n<td>50.39</td>\n<td>68.08</td>\n<td>77.01</td>\n<td>65.2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The abstract's \"+35% success over PBT\" cannot be reproduced from the tables; my own averages give +15.2 points or about +32% relative. The table shows something more interesting for the lineage: when FCP's recipe is moved to partial-view homes, FCP falls below PBT on two of the three tasks (34.07 and 30.00 against 56.88 and 56.08), so the borrowed idea does not transfer unchanged. These numbers are not comparable with FCP's own Overcooked results.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-habitat-3-0-a-simulator-in-which-a-person-can-be\">Habitat 3.0: a simulator in which a person can be the partner</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"108\" title=\"Habitat 3.0 · Habitat 3.0: A Co-Habitat for Humans, Avatars and Robots\">Habitat 3.0</button> (Puig et al., ICLR 2024) is a platform paper. It adds animated humanoid avatars (12 SMPL-X body shapes cached offline, so only skinning runs online) and a client-server HITL tool, with which a person drives an avatar by keyboard, mouse or VR. On one V100, a robot with a humanoid runs at 136 ± 8 frames per second in a single environment and 1191 ± 3 with 16 environments (the humanoid-only speed is 188 in the text and 155 in the appendix, which I could not reconcile).</p>\n<p>It brings two tasks. Social Navigation has the robot find and follow a humanoid at 1 to 2 metres (end-to-end RL 0.97 success against 1.00 for a map-using heuristic expert). Social Rearrangement is Social rearrange's task, made heterogeneous: a humanoid and a Spot robot. With 10 partners, about two thirds of them unseen in training, ZSC success is 71.79% for Plan-Pop3, 71.32 for Plan-Pop4 and 70.23 for Plan-Pop2, against about 50 for the single-partner policies (Learn-Single 50.94, Plan-Pop1 50.44) and 48.52 for the learned population. As I read it, the digit counts the partners trained with, so two to four training partners hold up with new partners where one does not (the single-policy learner's standard deviation is about 39 points).</p>\n<p>The human study is the more informative part. With 30 participants, relative efficiency (100 = person alone) is 133.80 for the single-policy robot and 123.46 for Plan-Pop3: both beat solo, and the two robots do not differ significantly. The robot that wins in simulated ZSC (Plan-Pop3) is therefore not the one with the higher mean with people, though that gap is not significant. Two caveats: avatars are scripted outside the HITL study, and the main runs use oracle low-level skills (learned skills drop one population's success from 77.79 to 41.96 in the text, 41.09 in the table). The text also says 71.7% where the table says 71.79. The inheritance edge from Social rearrange to Habitat 3.0 was added by the reading map from Habitat 3.0's own wording (it modifies the Social Rearrangement task of Szot et al.), not from the citation matching.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-partnr-let-language-models-write-the-tasks\">PARTNR: let language models write the tasks</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"112\" title=\"PARTNR · PARTNR: A Benchmark for Planning and Reasoning in Embodied Multi-agent Tasks\">PARTNR</button> (Chang et al., ICLR 2025) runs on Habitat 3.0's simulator and reuses its learned skills, hierarchical control and HITL tool. Its new thing is the task pipeline. An LLM writes an instruction, an object layout and an evaluation function for a parsed house; the simulator filters infeasible tasks; humans verify 1,000 seeds; and each seed then prompts about 100,000 tasks in other houses (37 training scenes), with 1,000 validation (13 scenes) and 1,000 test tasks (10 scenes). Four task types are covered: constraint-free, spatial, temporal and heterogeneous.</p>\n<p>The agents are a Spot robot and a simulated human, which is itself a ReAct-style Llama 3.1-70B agent; real participants number 129 in the HITL study. The robot lacks some actions (washing, switching things on), and planners get no privileged access to the partner's intent. Success rates (Table 2, decentralized unless stated; I cannot tell which split the table uses):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Planner or setting</th>\n<th>Success rate</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Heuristic expert, centralized, oracle skills, full observability</td>\n<td>0.84</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ReAct, oracle skills</td>\n<td>0.73</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fine-tuned Llama 8B</td>\n<td>0.70</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ReAct, learned skills</td>\n<td>0.57</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ReAct, learned skills and ConceptGraph perception</td>\n<td>0.30</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Human alone (Table 3)</td>\n<td>0.93</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>So the realistic LLM helper solves 30% where a person alone solves 93%. Coordination also has a cost: two LLM agents need 3,295 steps against 2,519 for one, and a human working with an LLM robot is slower than a human alone (3,443 or 4,268 steps against 3,047). The 0.30 row is 0.25 in the validation table, and on the test split the decentralized ReAct and fine-tuned rows read 0.63 and 0.51, so numbers depend on the split.</p>\n<h2 id=\"b-language-constraints-and-new-data\">Language, constraints and new data</h2>\n<p>The remaining papers are neither goal inferrers nor pool trainers. They ask what happens when the request is vague, when the partner is limited, when language models do the planning, and when we want real human recordings.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-handmethat-give-me-that-one\">HandMeThat: \"give me that one\"</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"104\" title=\"HandMeThat · HandMeThat: Human-Robot Communication in Physical and Social Environments\">HandMeThat</button> (Wan et al., NeurIPS 2022, Datasets and Benchmarks) puts the ambiguity in language. A simulated human acts toward a hidden goal, stops at a random step and makes a vague request (\"hand me that\"), whose words come from an RSA-based speaker model (a pragmatic-speaker model). The benchmark has 10,000 episodes and four levels: from unambiguous (L1), through social reasoning (L2) and goal inference plus pragmatics (L3), to inherently ambiguous (L4). The world is a text-only symbolic home with one request and no dialogue; clarification questions exist but are disabled for all agents. It cites <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"102\" title=\"Watch-And-Help · Watch-And-Help: A Challenge for Social Perception and Human-AI Collaboration\">WAH</button> only as a non-language task with a small goal space.</p>\n<p>In the partially observable setting (Table 2, 1,000 episodes, success %), a Seq2Seq baseline gets 25.5 on L1 falling to 3.9 on L3, while humans get 76 and 20 (25 episodes per level). A heuristic scores 94.9 on L1, but the table marks it as using true object states from the fully observable setting, so it is not a fair comparison. Oracles help unevenly: a goal oracle gives 7.4 against 3.9 on L3 but lowers L1 to 21.0. The authors flag that their speaker model uses the <em>true</em> goal as a prior, which makes some L3 tasks partly unsolvable. (The introduction says learned baselines get under 20%; Table 2 shows 25.5, which I use.)</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-holoassist-real-people-no-robot\">HoloAssist: real people, no robot</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"107\" title=\"HoloAssist · HoloAssist: an Egocentric Human Interaction Dataset for Interactive AI Assistants in the Real World\">HoloAssist</button> (Wang et al., ICCV 2023) is the only member that records real physical tasks. It is a dataset of 166 hours over 2,221 sessions, with 350 instructor-performer pairs and 222 participants, 20 object-centric tasks on 16 objects, and 7 sensor streams (RGB, depth, head pose, 3D hand pose, eye gaze, audio, IMU). A remote guide only speaks while watching live video; the performer wears a HoloLens 2. Annotations cover 414 coarse and 1,887 fine action classes, plus mistakes (about 6% of fine-grained actions) and interventions.</p>\n<p>The benchmarks are modest and say something. For mistake detection (F-score, on only 10% of the data), random gets 27.71, RGB 35.11, hands only 40.19, RGB plus hands 36.18 and adding gaze 32.08, so more modalities do not always help. For intervention type, precision is 13.93 for RGB, 37.54 for a pre-trained RGB model and 48.31 for RGB, hands and eyes. In hand forecasting, the model beats a static pose at 1.5 seconds (11.25 cm against 16.70) but not at 0.5 seconds (9.80 against 9.34). No robot, and no assistant that talks, appears in the paper; it joins the tree only as the real-world counterpart of the simulated helper benchmarks.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-coela-language-models-write-the-plan-and-the-mes\">CoELA: language models write the plan and the message</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"90\" title=\"CoELA · Building Cooperative Embodied Agents Modularly with Large Language Models\">CoELA</button> (Zhang et al., ICLR 2024, a guest from the <em>LLM planning for robot teams</em> tree) reuses WAH's environment and one of its planner baselines (the MCTS-based hierarchical planner, MHP). Its <em>Communicative Watch-And-Help</em> (C-WAH) adds messaging, and a second environment, TDW-MAT, uses RGB-D views with a trained Mask R-CNN. The method has five modules (perception, memory, communication, planning, execution). The language model only writes messages and picks high-level plans from a list; execution is hand-coded.</p>\n<p>On TDW-MAT (Table 1, transport rate), one agent alone gets 0.43, two RHP planners 0.61, an RHP planner plus CoELA 0.69, and two GPT-4 CoELAs 0.71, reported as a 39% efficiency improvement. A LLAMA-2 version reaches 0.53 and a variant called CoLLAMA 0.70. On C-WAH (Table 2, steps, lower is better), MHP alone takes 111, two MHPs 75, MHP plus CoELA 59 and two CoELAs 57 (reported as +49%; 1 − 57/111 is about 49%, my arithmetic, but I cannot reproduce the 39% from the TDW rows). In the human study with 8 subjects, trust is rated 6.3 with communication against 4.7 without. Caveats: CoELA's numbers are single runs (baselines 5 runs) on test sets of 24 (TDW-MAT) and 10 (C-WAH) episodes, and are not comparable with WAH's or <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"80\" title=\"NOPA · NOPA: Neurally-guided Online Probabilistic Assistance for Building Socially Intelligent Home Assistants\">NOPA</button>'s.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-chaic-help-a-partner-who-is-a-child-a-wheelchair\">CHAIC: help a partner who is a child, a wheelchair user or a cyclist</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"110\" title=\"CHAIC · Constrained Human-AI Cooperation: An Inclusive Embodied Social Intelligence Challenge\">CHAIC</button> (Du et al., NeurIPS 2024, Datasets and Benchmarks) joins the helper and LLM threads. It is a ThreeDWorld benchmark with four constrained agents (Child, Wheelchair, Bicycle, Frail) and eight tasks, where the helper sees only 512×512 egocentric RGB-D and neither the goal nor the limits. The partner is rule-based, with ground-truth segmentation and soft capability limits. The proposed helper, <strong>LLM+BM</strong>, is built inside the CoELA framework with a new behavior-modeling module: an action recognizer (TSN, 86.1% top-1 on 3,000 clips and 6 classes) grounds the partner's last action to an object, and GPT-4 picks the next plan. Baselines include a VLM helper (GPT-4o over the last 10 frames) and, in the appendix, a trained Smart Help helper.</p>\n<p>In transport rate (Table 2: no helper, LLM+BM, oracle), shopping is 0.37, 0.58 and 0.61; no constraint 0.53, 0.65 and 0.77; obstacle 0.07, 0.42 and 0.60. LLM+BM is best in 6 of 8 tasks (it loses obstacle to a planner baseline, 0.44, and one more task to the VLM helper, 0.70) and best in the efficiency measure in 7 of 8. The sharpest number is goal inference accuracy: 0.25 for LLM+BM against 0.88 for the oracle on the no-constraint task. There are only 12 test episodes per task, and the VLM helper can fall below no helper (0.26 against 0.28 on one task).</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-robofactory-from-helpers-to-many-arms\">RoboFactory: from helpers to many arms</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"99\" title=\"RoboFactory · RoboFactory: Exploring Embodied Agent Collaboration with Compositional Constraints\">RoboFactory</button> (Qin et al., ICCV 2025) joined this tree as a benchmark (the coordinator moved it from the LLM planning tree). It is about manipulation data rather than help: 11 tasks in ManiSkill with 1 to 4 Franka arms. GPT-4o proposes sub-goals together with logical, spatial and temporal constraints; a second GPT-4o call writes code that checks them against physical information (annotated grasp positions, a 5 cm voxel occupancy grid, occupancy over time); a violation halts execution and the reason goes back to the planner. Validated trajectories become the data (150 demos per task).</p>\n<p>The ablation covers 3 of 11 tasks: going from logical constraints only to all three, the valid-data rate rises from 80.2 to 97.5 (Lift Barrier), 62.5 to 98.9 (Three Robots Stack Cube) and 37.1 to 88.2 (Take Photo). A Diffusion Policy baseline with 150 demos averages 49% success with 1 agent, 27.5% with 2, 20.5% with 3 and 10% with 4 (and 0% on Long Pipeline Delivery); the baseline ignores other agents. Its only links here are table entries: it contrasts Watch-And-Help and <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"112\" title=\"PARTNR · PARTNR: A Benchmark for Planning and Reasoning in Embodied Multi-agent Tasks\">PARTNR</button> (2 agents, symbolic plans) with its own 4 arms and low-level control.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Paper</th>\n<th>Observation</th>\n<th>Partner is</th>\n<th>Skills</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>HandMeThat</td>\n<td>Text symbols</td>\n<td>Rational simulated human</td>\n<td>Symbolic actions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HoloAssist</td>\n<td>Egocentric video, 7 streams</td>\n<td>Real people</td>\n<td>No agent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CoELA</td>\n<td>Symbolic or RGB-D</td>\n<td>Another CoELA, planner or human</td>\n<td>Hand-coded execution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CHAIC</td>\n<td>Egocentric RGB-D</td>\n<td>Rule-based constrained agent</td>\n<td>A* execution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RoboFactory</td>\n<td>Global and per-arm cameras</td>\n<td>Other arms (baseline ignores them)</td>\n<td>Motion primitives</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PARTNR</td>\n<td>Habitat views</td>\n<td>LLM-simulated human; 129 real</td>\n<td>Learned or oracle</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"b-how-the-line-is-measured-and-why-the-numbers-do-\">How the line is measured, and why the numbers do not line up</h2>\n<p>The benchmarks are, in the tree's own words, \"each paper brings its own benchmark\". VirtualHome-Social serves <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"102\" title=\"Watch-And-Help · Watch-And-Help: A Challenge for Social Perception and Human-AI Collaboration\">WAH</button>, <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"80\" title=\"NOPA · NOPA: Neurally-guided Online Probabilistic Assistance for Building Socially Intelligent Home Assistants\">NOPA</button> and <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"90\" title=\"CoELA · Building Cooperative Embodied Agents Modularly with Large Language Models\">CoELA</button>'s C-WAH; AI2-THOR serves <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"85\" title=\"Smart Help · Smart Help: Strategic Opponent Modeling for Proactive and Adaptive Robot Assistance in Households\">Smart Help</button>; ThreeDWorld serves <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"110\" title=\"CHAIC · Constrained Human-AI Cooperation: An Inclusive Embodied Social Intelligence Challenge\">CHAIC</button> and CoELA's TDW-MAT; Habitat homes serve <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"106\" title=\"Social rearrange · Adaptive Coordination in Social Embodied Rearrangement\">Social rearrange</button>, <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"108\" title=\"Habitat 3.0 · Habitat 3.0: A Co-Habitat for Humans, Avatars and Robots\">Habitat 3.0</button> and <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"112\" title=\"PARTNR · PARTNR: A Benchmark for Planning and Reasoning in Embodied Multi-agent Tasks\">PARTNR</button>; <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"58\" title=\"Co-GAIL · Co-GAIL: Learning Diverse Strategies for Human-Robot Collaboration\">Co-GAIL</button>, <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"104\" title=\"HandMeThat · HandMeThat: Human-Robot Communication in Physical and Social Environments\">HandMeThat</button>, <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"107\" title=\"HoloAssist · HoloAssist: an Egocentric Human Interaction Dataset for Interactive AI Assistants in the Real World\">HoloAssist</button> and <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"99\" title=\"RoboFactory · RoboFactory: Exploring Embodied Agent Collaboration with Compositional Constraints\">RoboFactory</button> have their own tasks or data. Here is what each headline number measures, so that no two get confused:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Paper</th>\n<th>Metric and conditions</th>\n<th>Headline as reported</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Co-GAIL</td>\n<td>Replay success, 3 tasks, 3 seeds</td>\n<td>53.3 / 43.9 / 40.0% vs 21.1 / 13.3 / 15.0%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"59\" title=\"FCP · Collaborating with Humans without Human Data\">FCP</button></td>\n<td>Overcooked deliveries, human proxy</td>\n<td>10.6 vs 4.7 without checkpoints</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Watch-And-Help</td>\n<td>Goal-inference precision / recall (my pick)</td>\n<td>0.85 / 0.96 on test 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HandMeThat</td>\n<td>Success %, partially observable</td>\n<td>25.5 (L1) to 3.9 (L3); humans 76 and 20</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NOPA</td>\n<td>Speedup, read off plots</td>\n<td>about 0.44 vs about 0.34</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Social rearrange</td>\n<td>ZSC success, unseen partners</td>\n<td>average 63.0 vs 47.8 for PBT (my average)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HoloAssist</td>\n<td>Mistake-detection F-score, 10% of data</td>\n<td>40.19 (hands) vs 35.11 (RGB)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Smart Help</td>\n<td>Task success, 14 cases</td>\n<td>48.3% vs 34.5% (LLM); oracle 59.3%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CoELA</td>\n<td>Transport rate; C-WAH steps</td>\n<td>0.71 vs 0.43 alone; 57 vs 111 steps</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Habitat 3.0</td>\n<td>Efficiency relative to solo, 30 people</td>\n<td>123.46 to 133.80 (solo = 100)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CHAIC</td>\n<td>Transport rate, shopping</td>\n<td>0.58 vs 0.37 alone; oracle 0.61</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RoboFactory</td>\n<td>Valid-data rate, Take Photo</td>\n<td>37.1% to 88.2%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PARTNR</td>\n<td>Success rate, learned skills and perception</td>\n<td>0.30 vs 0.93 for a human alone</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The unit, the partner, the number of test episodes and the simulator all differ from row to row, so I do not rank any row against another. A few reading rules follow from the notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A \"speedup\" or \"efficiency\" is relative to that paper's own solo baseline</strong> (episode-length ratio for WAH and NOPA; person alone = 100 for Habitat 3.0).</li>\n<li><strong>The partner decides what a number means.</strong> It is a planner (NOPA), a scripted expert (Smart Help), a rule-based agent (CHAIC), a scripted avatar (Habitat 3.0) or an LLM (PARTNR); success with one need not carry over to a person.</li>\n<li><strong>Test sets are small.</strong> 14 cases (Smart Help), 12 episodes per task (CHAIC), 24 and 10 episodes (CoELA), 100 episodes (WAH, NOPA).</li>\n<li><strong>Perfect skills hide errors.</strong> Habitat 3.0's main runs use oracle skills, and learned skills nearly halve one population's success (77.79 to 41.96).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>My reading is a funnel: the social part works in the simplest settings (symbolic views, oracle skills, scripted partners) and degrades as realism is added (0.25 goal accuracy from camera views in CHAIC, 0.30 against a human's 0.93 in PARTNR, 10% success with four arms in RoboFactory).</p>\n<h2 id=\"b-open-problems-and-a-reading-order\">Open problems, and a reading order</h2>\n<h3 id=\"b-what-is-still-open\">What is still open</h3>\n<p>In rough order of how much they worry me:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Partners are not people.</strong> Most partners are scripts, planners, trained pools or LLMs. Human studies exist (8 people for <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"90\" title=\"CoELA · Building Cooperative Embodied Agents Modularly with Large Language Models\">CoELA</button>, 10 for <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"80\" title=\"NOPA · NOPA: Neurally-guided Online Probabilistic Assistance for Building Socially Intelligent Home Assistants\">NOPA</button>, 30 for <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"108\" title=\"Habitat 3.0 · Habitat 3.0: A Co-Habitat for Humans, Avatars and Robots\">Habitat 3.0</button>, 114 for <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"59\" title=\"FCP · Collaborating with Humans without Human Data\">FCP</button>, 129 for <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"112\" title=\"PARTNR · PARTNR: A Benchmark for Planning and Reasoning in Embodied Multi-agent Tasks\">PARTNR</button>) but are small or secondary.</li>\n<li><strong>Goal inference from camera views is far from solved.</strong> <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"110\" title=\"CHAIC · Constrained Human-AI Cooperation: An Inclusive Embodied Social Intelligence Challenge\">CHAIC</button>'s 0.25 against 0.88 for the oracle, and <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"102\" title=\"Watch-And-Help · Watch-And-Help: A Challenge for Social Perception and Human-AI Collaboration\">WAH</button>'s drop from 0.85 / 0.96 to 0.68 / 0.64 when two activities are mixed, show that goal reading is brittle once the world is richer.</li>\n<li><strong>Oracle skills and symbolic views hide errors.</strong> Habitat 3.0's main runs and <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"106\" title=\"Social rearrange · Adaptive Coordination in Social Embodied Rearrangement\">Social rearrange</button>'s setup use perfect skills, and PARTNR's fall from 0.57 to 0.30 shows what real perception does.</li>\n<li><strong>The two threads rarely meet.</strong> In the thirteen papers I read, goal-inference methods (WAH, NOPA, <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"85\" title=\"Smart Help · Smart Help: Strategic Opponent Modeling for Proactive and Adaptive Robot Assistance in Households\">Smart Help</button>) and partner-pool methods (FCP, Social rearrange, Habitat 3.0) are not run on one benchmark; the only cross-use I saw is CHAIC training a Smart Help helper in its appendix, and Smart Help's reuse of WAH's planners as baselines.</li>\n<li><strong>No shared yardstick.</strong> Each helper paper reports its own metric on 10 to 100 test episodes.</li>\n<li><strong>More agents cost more.</strong> Four arms bring <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"99\" title=\"RoboFactory · RoboFactory: Exploring Embodied Agent Collaboration with Compositional Constraints\">RoboFactory</button>'s baseline from 49% to 10%, and in PARTNR two LLM agents take longer than one and a human is slowed down by an LLM robot.</li>\n<li><strong>Real data and robots are missing from the helper side.</strong> <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"107\" title=\"HoloAssist · HoloAssist: an Egocentric Human Interaction Dataset for Interactive AI Assistants in the Real World\">HoloAssist</button> has real people and no robot; <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"58\" title=\"Co-GAIL · Co-GAIL: Learning Diverse Strategies for Human-Robot Collaboration\">Co-GAIL</button> has no real-robot result; Smart Help has no human study.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"b-if-you-only-have-an-afternoon\">If you only have an afternoon</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"102\" title=\"Watch-And-Help · Watch-And-Help: A Challenge for Social Perception and Human-AI Collaboration\">Watch-And-Help</button> for the problem and the platform: it is the root of the helping chain.</li>\n<li><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"80\" title=\"NOPA · NOPA: Neurally-guided Online Probabilistic Assistance for Building Socially Intelligent Home Assistants\">NOPA</button> for online goal inference, then <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"85\" title=\"Smart Help · Smart Help: Strategic Opponent Modeling for Proactive and Adaptive Robot Assistance in Households\">Smart Help</button> for the capability twist.</li>\n<li><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"106\" title=\"Social rearrange · Adaptive Coordination in Social Embodied Rearrangement\">Social rearrange</button> for how partner pools move into 3D homes (read <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"59\" title=\"FCP · Collaborating with Humans without Human Data\">FCP</button> first if you do not know the recipe, or the sibling population-training post).</li>\n<li><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"108\" title=\"Habitat 3.0 · Habitat 3.0: A Co-Habitat for Humans, Avatars and Robots\">Habitat 3.0</button> for the platform and its human study, where the simulation winner does not have the higher mean with people.</li>\n<li><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"112\" title=\"PARTNR · PARTNR: A Benchmark for Planning and Reasoning in Embodied Multi-agent Tasks\">PARTNR</button> for the current benchmark, read together with <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"110\" title=\"CHAIC · Constrained Human-AI Cooperation: An Inclusive Embodied Social Intelligence Challenge\">CHAIC</button> for the limits of LLM helpers.</li>\n<li><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"104\" title=\"HandMeThat · HandMeThat: Human-Robot Communication in Physical and Social Environments\">HandMeThat</button> and <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"107\" title=\"HoloAssist · HoloAssist: an Egocentric Human Interaction Dataset for Interactive AI Assistants in the Real World\">HoloAssist</button> if you care about language or real recordings.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The sibling lines on LLM planning for robot teams (where <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"90\" title=\"CoELA · Building Cooperative Embodied Agents Modularly with Large Language Models\">CoELA</button> lives) and on population training are covered in their own posts; for the field's vocabulary start with the <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"4\" title=\"AHT survey · A Survey of Ad Hoc Teamwork Research\">AHT survey</button> and the <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"0\" title=\"AHT position · Ad Hoc Autonomous Agent Teams: Collaboration without Pre-Coordination\">AHT position</button> paper.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-how-these-numbers-were-handled\">How these numbers were handled</h3>\n<p>Every number above comes from the paper it is attributed to. NOPA's values are read off plots (about ±0.02) and marked with \"about\"; I flagged my own arithmetic (96 partners, averages for Social rearrange, the +15.2 points, the 1 − 57/111, NOPA's 0.10 difference) and places where a paper disagrees with itself (Social rearrange's abstract, Habitat 3.0's 71.7 versus 71.79, PARTNR's 0.30 versus 0.25, HandMeThat's intro). Venue and year follow the file name of each PDF; NOPA's and Smart Help's venues are not printed in their arXiv PDFs. Proofs were not checked, and human-study sizes are as reported by the papers.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-references\">References</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Stone et al., 2010. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Ad%20Hoc%20Autonomous%20Agent%20Teams%3A%20Collaboration%20without%20Pre-Coordination\">Ad Hoc Autonomous Agent Teams: Collaboration without Pre-Coordination</a>. <em>AAAI 2010</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"0\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Wang et al., 2021. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Co-GAIL%3A%20Learning%20Diverse%20Strategies%20for%20Human-Robot%20Collaboration\">Co-GAIL: Learning Diverse Strategies for Human-Robot Collaboration</a>. <em>CoRL 2021</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"58\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Strouse et al., 2021. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Collaborating%20with%20Humans%20without%20Human%20Data\">Collaborating with Humans without Human Data</a>. <em>NeurIPS 2021</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"59\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Puig et al., 2021. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Watch-And-Help%3A%20A%20Challenge%20for%20Social%20Perception%20and%20Human-AI%20Collaboration\">Watch-And-Help: A Challenge for Social Perception and Human-AI Collaboration</a>. <em>ICLR 2021</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"102\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Mirsky et al., 2022. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=A%20Survey%20of%20Ad%20Hoc%20Teamwork%20Research\">A Survey of Ad Hoc Teamwork Research</a>. <em>EUMAS 2022</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"4\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Wan et al., 2022. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=HandMeThat%3A%20Human-Robot%20Communication%20in%20Physical%20and%20Social%20Environments\">HandMeThat: Human-Robot Communication in Physical and Social Environments</a>. <em>NeurIPS 2022</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"104\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Puig et al., 2023. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=NOPA%3A%20Neurally-guided%20Online%20Probabilistic%20Assistance%20for%20Building%20Socially%20Intelligent%20Home%20Assistants\">NOPA: Neurally-guided Online Probabilistic Assistance for Building Socially Intelligent Home Assistants</a>. <em>ICRA 2023</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"80\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Szot et al., 2023. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Adaptive%20Coordination%20in%20Social%20Embodied%20Rearrangement\">Adaptive Coordination in Social Embodied Rearrangement</a>. <em>ICML 2023</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"106\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Wang et al., 2023. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=HoloAssist%3A%20an%20Egocentric%20Human%20Interaction%20Dataset%20for%20Interactive%20AI%20Assistants%20in%20the%20Real%20World\">HoloAssist: an Egocentric Human Interaction Dataset for Interactive AI Assistants in the Real World</a>. <em>ICCV 2023</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"107\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Cao et al., 2024. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Smart%20Help%3A%20Strategic%20Opponent%20Modeling%20for%20Proactive%20and%20Adaptive%20Robot%20Assistance%20in%20Households\">Smart Help: Strategic Opponent Modeling for Proactive and Adaptive Robot Assistance in Households</a>. <em>arXiv 2024</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"85\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Zhang et al., 2024. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Building%20Cooperative%20Embodied%20Agents%20Modularly%20with%20Large%20Language%20Models\">Building Cooperative Embodied Agents Modularly with Large Language Models</a>. <em>ICLR 2024</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"90\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Puig et al., 2024. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Habitat%203.0%3A%20A%20Co-Habitat%20for%20Humans%2C%20Avatars%20and%20Robots\">Habitat 3.0: A Co-Habitat for Humans, Avatars and Robots</a>. <em>ICLR 2024</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"108\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Du et al., 2024. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Constrained%20Human-AI%20Cooperation%3A%20An%20Inclusive%20Embodied%20Social%20Intelligence%20Challenge\">Constrained Human-AI Cooperation: An Inclusive Embodied Social Intelligence Challenge</a>. <em>NeurIPS 2024</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"110\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Qin et al., 2025. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=RoboFactory%3A%20Exploring%20Embodied%20Agent%20Collaboration%20with%20Compositional%20Constraints\">RoboFactory: Exploring Embodied Agent Collaboration with Compositional Constraints</a>. <em>ICCV 2025</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"99\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Chang et al., 2025. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=PARTNR%3A%20A%20Benchmark%20for%20Planning%20and%20Reasoning%20in%20Embodied%20Multi-agent%20Tasks\">PARTNR: A Benchmark for Planning and Reasoning in Embodied Multi-agent Tasks</a>. <em>ICLR 2025</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"112\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n</ol></div>");
